{{!-- Hash parameter: urlRequestingTls, the DATABASE_URL setting that requests TLS without NODE_ENV. --}}
{{#if (has "stack" "nextjs")}}
- Next.js: `next build` replaces `process.env.NODE_ENV` with `production` in the server bundle, so every deployment of the build uses TLS.
{{/if}}
{{#if (has "stack" "tanstack-start")}}
- TanStack Start: `vite build` replaces it in the server bundle the same way.
{{/if}}
{{#if (has "stack" "hono")}}
- Hono: Bun runs the source, so the client reads the variable at runtime. The `start` script sets it (`NODE_ENV=production bun run src/index.ts`){{#if (has "deployment" "sst")}}, and `sst.config.ts` sets it in the Lambda function `environment` (`development` under `sst dev`){{/if}}{{#if (has "deployment" "cloudflare")}}, and `wrangler deploy` replaces it in the Worker bundle{{/if}}.
{{/if}}
{{#if (has "stack" "node")}}
- Node: Bun runs the source too, and the `start` script sets it the same way.
{{/if}}
{{#if (or (has "stack" "hono") (has "stack" "node"))}}

Any other production entry of a Bun app ({{#if (has "deployment" "terraform-aws")}}the Lambda you create next to `infra/`, {{/if}}a container or a process manager running `bun src/index.ts`) must set `NODE_ENV=production` itself, or `DATABASE_URL` must set {{urlRequestingTls}}. A bundle made with `bun build` (a Lambda zip, for instance) gets `process.env.NODE_ENV` inlined at build time instead: a plain `bun build` inlines `development` and turns the TLS default off whatever the runtime environment says, so build it with `bun build --production` or `NODE_ENV=production bun build`.
{{/if}}
